  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24–48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">

      <p><strong>BRICS Summit New Delhi, September 11–13.</strong> The BRICS summit in New Delhi (Sep 11–13) produced a joint declaration on restraint, signed by Iran, UAE, and China. Pezeshkian held a bilateral meeting with the Abu Dhabi Crown Prince — the first since the February conflict onset. Modi met Pezeshkian and called for dialogue. Pezeshkian stated Iran "would not surrender to the United States." No Iran-USA bilateral session occurred at the summit or on its margins, and no mediator confirmed any formal engagement between US and Iranian principals during the three-day event. → moved <span class="cite">F3</span></p>

      <p><strong>Salalah Ministerial Collapse, September 13.</strong> Omani FM Badr Albusaidi announced the indefinite postponement of the Salalah ministerial (six GCC states plus Iran, focused on the Hormuz corridor) on September 13. Saudi Arabia had submitted proposed amendments; Bahrain declined to attend. No rescheduled date was set. The Salalah format had been the principal near-term vehicle for a partial Hormuz transit arrangement. → moved <span class="cite">F4</span></p>

      <p><strong>El Gaia Strike and IRGC Toll Regime, September 12.</strong> At approximately 2300 UTC on September 12, a vessel was struck near Limah, Oman. UKMTO confirmed the incident: El Gaia, a Panama-flagged products tanker with Indian crew, caught fire; the crew was evacuated and one crew member remains missing. The IRGC "Persian Gulf Strait Authority" (PGSA) toll regime has been formally documented: per-vessel fees of up to $2M payable in yuan, Bitcoin, or USDT. IMF PortWatch records 6 transits September 6 and 7 transits September 9. Lloyd's List: no Western-allied transits since May 4. → supports <span class="cite">F4</span></p>

      <p><strong>Israeli Polling, September 8–9.</strong> Five post-candidate-list-closure polls were conducted September 8–9. Yashar (Eisenkot) leads in four of five: Channel 12 (Yashar 25, Likud 21), KAN News (Yashar 25, Likud 21), Haaretz (Yashar increases lead, opposition bloc at 57 seats), Channel 13 (Yashar 22, Likud 19). One outlier only: i24NEWS/Direct Polls (Likud 27, Yashar 22). The Netanyahu bloc registers approximately 51–52 seats across most surveys, well below the 61-seat governing threshold. → moved <span class="cite">F5</span></p>

      <p><strong>Araghchi Precondition and CENTCOM Quiet, September 13–14.</strong> Araghchi stated on September 13 that the US must abide by Islamabad MOU commitments before Hormuz reopens — an explicit precondition, not an offer to negotiate. CENTCOM recorded five consecutive days without published kinetic action (Days 195–199, September 10–14). Neither the State Department nor any named mediator confirmed a formal Iran-US session during the five-day quiet period. → supports <span class="cite">F3</span></p>

    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 4. SCENARIO MAP ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="scenario-map">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Scenario Map
      <span class="meta">Computed from Forecast Board · 1 sentence per scenario</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="scenario-map">
      <div class="prob-bar-container">
        <div class="prob-bar prob-escalation" style="width:25%;">Escalation 25%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-status" style="width:67%;">Protracted 67%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-deescalation" style="width:8%;">De-esc 8%</div>
      </div>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path (25%):</strong> Structural drivers — IRGC PGSA enforcement (El Gaia precedent), no diplomatic track open (F3=0.06, F4=0.06), F8 failing (0.82 probability) — leave no exit; CENTCOM 5-day quiet reduces acute risk vs. v48.0's 65% but institutionalized toll regime and inoperative corridor mechanism sustain structural pressure.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path (67%):</strong> Base case: F4=0.06 (Hormuz remains mostly closed), F3=0.06 (no near-term session), Araghchi precondition intact, Salalah inoperative — operational quiet preserves stalemate as the modal outcome.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path (8%):</strong> F8 (Iran-US session by Oct 15) at p=0.18 is the sole structural driver; P(de-esc) ≈ F8 × P(meaningful de-esc trajectory|session) ≈ 0.18 × 0.45 ≈ 0.08; F9 (MOU by Nov 30) at 0.12 provides a tail probability beyond the 30-day horizon.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>

      <p>Iranian state media (IRNA, Tasnim, PressTV, state television) framed the BRICS summit declaration as international vindication of Tehran's position. The Abu Dhabi Crown Prince bilateral was framed as normalization from a position of strength; the BRICS joint declaration on restraint was presented as confirmation of US and Israeli isolation. Araghchi's MOU precondition statement was carried verbatim by IRNA and framed as a firm condition — no language of softening appeared in any official state outlet. The Salalah postponement received brief coverage; attribution went to Saudi "obstruction" with no acknowledgment of Iranian preconditions as a factor. → supports <span class="cite">F3</span></p>

      <p>The El Gaia strike was covered without IRGC attribution; the vessel was described as having operated on an "illegal route" — consistent with the PGSA enforcement frame. State television framed the 7-vessel AIS transit figure as "effective deterrence." The CENTCOM five-day quiet appeared in Kayhan and Javan editorials framed as "a de facto admission of military limits." No state outlet acknowledged the formal documentation of PGSA fees or the El Gaia crew casualty. → supports <span class="cite">F4</span></p>

    </div>
  </div>

